Analysis: NCERT Class 12 India: People and Economy (Chapter 1: Population, pp. 5-6) delineates four distinct phases:
1. Phase I (1901–1921): Period of stagnant or stationary phase of growth (high birth and death rates, negative growth in 1911-1921) (A);
2. Phase II (1921–1951): Period of steady population growth due to improvements in sanitation and health (B);
3. Phase III (1951–1981): Period of population explosion due to rapid mortality decline while fertility remained high (C);
4. Phase IV (Post-1981 to present): Period of high growth with definite signs of slowing down due to declining crude birth rate (D).
Thus, the chronological order is (A), (B), (C), (D).

